# MLServe: Batching and Caching

```elixir
Mix.install([
  # Running from a clone of the repo. Against the published package this is:
  #   {:ml_serve, "~> 0.1.0"}
  {:ml_serve, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
  {:kino, "~> 0.14"}
])
```

## Two different ways to not do work

Batching and caching both make inference cheaper, but they solve opposite problems.

**Batching** helps when many callers each hold *one* input and arrive at nearly the same moment.
A GPU that processes 32 rows in barely more time than one row is being wasted by a pool feeding
it a row at a time. Batching amortises the fixed cost — the NIF boundary, the host-to-device
copy, the HTTP round trip — across a window of arrivals.

**Caching** helps when the *same* input arrives more than once. It does no work at all rather
than cheaper work, which is why it is off by default: caching a prediction is only correct when
the same input must produce the same output, and that is false the moment a model reads a clock,
a random seed, or mutable feature state.

## A backend that counts its own invocations

`load/1` runs once and its state is shared by every worker, so a `:counters` reference created
there is a lock-free counter all of them write to — the same trick a real backend uses to hold a
single NIF resource across a pool.

```elixir
defmodule CountingModel do
  @behaviour MLServe.Model

  @impl true
  def capabilities, do: %{concurrency: :exclusive, load: :once}

  @impl true
  def load(_config), do: {:ok, :counters.new(1, [:write_concurrency])}

  @impl true
  def predict(counters, input) do
    :counters.add(counters, 1, 1)
    # Stand-in for the fixed cost every real backend pays whether handed one row or a thousand.
    Process.sleep(5)
    {:ok, input * 2}
  end

  # The optional callback that makes batching worth anything: one invocation, one fixed cost,
  # N rows. Without it MLServe still batches, but falls back to mapping predict/2 over the list.
  @impl true
  def batch_predict(counters, inputs) do
    :counters.add(counters, 1, 1)
    Process.sleep(5)
    {:ok, Enum.map(inputs, &(&1 * 2))}
  end
end
```

## Watching the window

A batch flushes when `:max_size` inputs have accumulated (`reason: :full`) or `:timeout`
milliseconds have passed since the **first** input in the batch (`reason: :timeout`). Timing from
the first arrival rather than the last bounds the added latency at `:timeout` for every caller; a
sliding window timed from the last arrival can starve the earliest caller indefinitely under
steady traffic.

```elixir
defmodule FlushTally do
  @table :flush_tally

  def start do
    :ets.new(@table, [:public, :named_table])

    :telemetry.attach_many(
      "batch-flushes",
      [[:ml_serve, :batch, :flush]],
      &__MODULE__.handle_event/4,
      nil
    )
  end

  def handle_event(_event, %{size: size}, %{reason: reason}, _config) do
    :ets.update_counter(@table, :flushes, {2, 1}, {:flushes, 0})
    :ets.update_counter(@table, :rows, {2, size}, {:rows, 0})
    :ets.update_counter(@table, reason, {2, 1}, {reason, 0})
    if size > get(:largest), do: :ets.insert(@table, {:largest, size})
    :ok
  end

  def get(key) do
    case :ets.lookup(@table, key) do
      [{^key, value}] -> value
      [] -> 0
    end
  end
end

FlushTally.start()
```

Two identical models. The only difference is the window.

```elixir
{:ok, _} = MLServe.load_model(:unbatched, backend: CountingModel, workers: 4)

{:ok, _} =
  MLServe.load_model(:batched,
    backend: CountingModel,
    workers: 4,
    batching: [max_size: 16, timeout: 10]
  )

:ok = MLServe.await_ready()
```

## 200 concurrent callers

```elixir
run = fn model ->
  {elapsed_us, _} =
    :timer.tc(fn ->
      1..200
      |> Task.async_stream(&MLServe.predict!(model, &1), max_concurrency: 200, timeout: 30_000)
      |> Stream.run()
    end)

  Float.round(elapsed_us / 1_000, 1)
end

unbatched_ms = run.(:unbatched)
batched_ms = run.(:batched)

Kino.DataTable.new([
  %{setup: "no batching", wall_clock: "#{unbatched_ms}ms", backend_calls: 200},
  %{setup: "batching", wall_clock: "#{batched_ms}ms", backend_calls: FlushTally.get(:flushes)},
  %{setup: "speedup", wall_clock: "#{Float.round(unbatched_ms / batched_ms, 1)}×", backend_calls: nil}
])
```

## How the window actually behaved

```elixir
flushes = FlushTally.get(:flushes)
rows = FlushTally.get(:rows)

Kino.DataTable.new([
  %{measure: "rows processed", value: rows},
  %{measure: "flushes", value: flushes},
  %{measure: "average batch", value: Float.round(rows / flushes, 1)},
  %{measure: "largest batch", value: FlushTally.get(:largest)},
  %{measure: "flushed on :full", value: FlushTally.get(:full)},
  %{measure: "flushed on :timeout", value: FlushTally.get(:timeout)}
])
```

Note that the largest batch exceeds `max_size: 16`. That is not a bug, and it is the part worth
understanding: `:max_size` is the trigger that *opens* a flush, not a cap on what the flush
carries — a flush takes everything queued at that instant.

In-flight batches are separately capped at the worker count. When every worker is busy, flushes
are held and arrivals keep accumulating, and that hold *is* the backpressure. So a burst
naturally produces larger batches exactly when larger batches pay off most.

Mostly `:full` is the healthy signature. Mostly `:timeout` means you are paying the whole window
in latency for batches that never fill: shorten it, or accept smaller batches.

## Caching

```elixir
defmodule CacheTally do
  def start do
    :ets.new(:cache_tally, [:public, :named_table])

    :telemetry.attach_many(
      "cache-events",
      [[:ml_serve, :cache, :hit], [:ml_serve, :cache, :miss]],
      &__MODULE__.handle_event/4,
      nil
    )
  end

  def handle_event([:ml_serve, :cache, outcome], _measurements, _metadata, _config) do
    :ets.update_counter(:cache_tally, outcome, {2, 1}, {outcome, 0})
    :ok
  end

  def get(key) do
    case :ets.lookup(:cache_tally, key) do
      [{^key, value}] -> value
      [] -> 0
    end
  end

  def reset, do: :ets.delete_all_objects(:cache_tally)
end

CacheTally.start()
```

Off unless you ask. The same call, with and without:

```elixir
time = fn fun ->
  {us, _} = :timer.tc(fun)
  Float.round(us / 1_000, 1)
end

CacheTally.reset()

uncached_1 = time.(fn -> MLServe.predict!(:batched, 7) end)
uncached_2 = time.(fn -> MLServe.predict!(:batched, 7) end)

cached_1 = time.(fn -> MLServe.predict!(:batched, 99, cache: true) end)
cached_2 = time.(fn -> MLServe.predict!(:batched, 99, cache: true) end)
cached_3 = time.(fn -> MLServe.predict!(:batched, 99, cache: true) end)

Kino.DataTable.new([
  %{call: "no :cache, 1st", ms: uncached_1},
  %{call: "no :cache, 2nd", ms: uncached_2},
  %{call: "cache: true, 1st", ms: cached_1},
  %{call: "cache: true, 2nd", ms: cached_2},
  %{call: "cache: true, 3rd", ms: cached_3}
])
```

```elixir
%{hits: CacheTally.get(:hit), misses: CacheTally.get(:miss)}
```

## Keys, TTL, and what is never cached

The default key is SHA-256 over `:erlang.term_to_binary(input, [:deterministic])`. The
`:deterministic` flag matters: without it, large maps serialise in internal-hash order and two
equal inputs can produce different binaries, silently halving the hit rate.

For a large tensor that hash costs more than the inference it saves. When you already hold
something that identifies the input, say so with `:cache_key` — and understand that it is a
promise MLServe takes at face value.

```elixir
CacheTally.reset()

# Two genuinely different inputs, one key. The second returns the first one's answer.
MLServe.predict!(:batched, 1, cache: true, cache_key: "account:42")
second = MLServe.predict!(:batched, 1_000, cache: true, cache_key: "account:42")

%{
  input: 1_000,
  returned: second,
  hits: CacheTally.get(:hit),
  note: "2 × 1_000 would be 2_000 — this is the cached answer for input 1"
}
```

Errors are never cached: a transient backend failure must not be pinned for the whole TTL.

```elixir
{:ok, _} =
  MLServe.load_model(:flaky,
    backend: MLServe.Backend.Static,
    config: [error: :upstream_unavailable]
  )

:ok = MLServe.await_ready(:flaky)
CacheTally.reset()

for _ <- 1..3, do: MLServe.predict(:flaky, :anything)

%{failing_calls: 3, cache_hits: CacheTally.get(:hit)}
```

## Cleaning up

```elixir
for model <- MLServe.models(), do: MLServe.unload_model(model)
MLServe.models()
```

